# A developer's card, never a shipped one — and where the build box goes.
#
# Chassis-owned and rewritten by every render; `config.local` beside it is
# yours and git-ignored. Unless `[targets.image]` in appliance.toml names a
# key, the rendered `config` ships no shell account and no SSH, so the only way
# to reach the control socket — and so to make the first administrator — is
# from a console nobody can log in to. Copy this to
# `config.local`, which `build-image.sh` appends after `config`, put your own
# public key in it, and the card you write can be signed into over SSH as root.
#
# A key and never a password: root has no password to guess, and sshd's
# default lets root in by key and no other way.

ENABLE_SSH=1
PUBKEY_SSH_ROOT='ssh-ed25519 AAAA… you@host'

# A key here names the image `<name>-dev`, which also keys pi-gen's work
# directory: a developer's build and a release build keep two rootfs trees.

# The unprivileged alternative, which the stage also supports: a first user
# with a key, a login shell, and passwordless sudo for the socket. Not
# `operator`, `staff` or `users` — Debian base groups, which `adduser` refuses
# as user names.
#
# FIRST_USER_NAME='dev'
# PUBKEY_SSH_FIRST_USER='ssh-ed25519 AAAA… you@host'
# PUBKEY_ONLY_SSH=1
# PASSWORDLESS_SUDO=1

# The product's Tailscale auth key: with `[targets.image.tailscale]` in
# appliance.toml, every card written from the image tries to join the
# product's tailnet at every boot, and every five minutes within one, until it
# does, then deletes the key. **A secret**, and so is every image built with
# it. It does not name the image `<name>-dev`: a release carries it.
#
# Make the key for the image, not for one card: reusable, tagged, and expiring
# after the last card written from the image has first booted. Tailscale's
# admin console caps an auth key at 90 days; once the key expires, the image
# must be rebuilt with a new one. Log upload to Tailscale stays off either way.
#
# build-remote.sh also takes it from its environment, where it wins over this
# file, as in `op run --env-file=.env -- scaffold/image/build-remote.sh`; a
# local build-image.sh refuses one from the environment.
#
# TAILSCALE_AUTH_KEY=''

# The Linux box `build-remote.sh` and `clean.sh --remote` reach over ssh.
# pi-gen wants Debian, root, git and loop devices, and `build-remote.sh` wants
# sudo, rsync and pgrep there; an x86_64 host builds the arm64 image under
# qemu-user-static, a spare Pi 5 builds it natively and faster. Tens of
# gigabytes free. `BUILD_DIR` is an absolute path other than `/`, of letters,
# digits, `.`, `_`, `/` and `-`, with no `.`, `..` or empty component; an IPv6
# box wants a Host alias in ~/.ssh/config. All three may come from the
# environment instead, where they win over this file.
#
# BUILD_HOST=build.example.invalid
# BUILD_USER=root
# BUILD_DIR=/root/wiretap-appliance
